A||||21||nette||GT vs FORMULA||||1||
Z||000000||nette||11-15-2001||03:19 PM||||I'm looking at buying another Fiero very soon.  I wanted to know what opinions are out there regarding GT vs FORMULA.  Which do you prefer and why?  Thanks!<P>nette||||reg||1
Z||000001||JohnF||11-15-2001||03:23 PM||||Everyone has an opinion and mine is that this discussion goes in chat!||||reg||8
Z||000002||nette||11-15-2001||03:50 PM||||Well excuuuuuuuuse me!||||reg||1
Z||000003||FieroFanaticus2||11-15-2001||04:10 PM||||LOL  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000004||Monkeyman||11-15-2001||04:13 PM||||This really does belong in Gen Chat, but here's my opinion on GT vs Formula.  I like eye candy & options so I'd go with the GT.  The Formula is just a very stripped down version of the GT without the fastback/aero body.  The only thing the Formula has going for it (IMO) is the GT drivetrain and the suspension.  Since almost any 15 year old suspension will be worn out, you'll have to replace parts no matter what.  It's more expensive to upgrade the '88 suspension.  Since '88s are more expensive, I'd buy an '86/'87 GT (both fastbacks), upgrade the suspension and eat stock '88s for lunch.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000005||Raydar||11-15-2001||04:23 PM||||"And a warm welcome to you, too, John."  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/rolleyes.gif"><P>The 88 GT and Formula are mechanically identical. Both have the "redesigned for 88" suspension and brakes. Only difference is the body and option content. The Formula is just a bit lighter. Should be a bit faster than the GT, but in the real world I don't know if you could tell the difference. <BR>88 GTs are quite a bit more expensive than Formulas. <BR>Up through 87 GTs have the 1st design suspension and brakes. Ride a bit rougher than the 88s, and don't stop or handle quite as well without mods. (Some may argue this.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> ) The pre-88 cars are generally less expensive, but are much easier to find suspension and brake parts for. <BR>All according to what you want. <BR>Happy hunting! <BR>And welcome to the Forum.<P>------------------<BR>Raydar<BR><FONT SIZE="1"><BR>From the Department of Redundancy Department.</FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000006||MinnGreenGT||11-15-2001||04:25 PM||||I agree with MM... go with a GT.  '88 if you've got extra cash for higher-ticket replacement parts, or '86-'87 for a "budget."<P>------------------<BR>              <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/GreenGT6.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000007||Turbo88||11-15-2001||04:30 PM||||I am 180* off from The Monkeyman! I don't care about eye candy and wish the days where you could buy a stripped out car with nothing more than the go-fast parts were here again! That said, I believe the 88 Formula is mechanically identical to the 88 GT except it has the notchback body. Here is why I choose a formula... The formula has a curb weight of 2473lbs. The 88 GT weighs 2790lbs at the curb. If you go by the good 'ol hotrodder rule of thumb, of 10hp or 100lbs = .10 fast in the 1/4, that means you have a .3s advantage right out of the box. The same applies to road racing where you will be fast on acceleration, deceleration, and cornering. But the flip side is this. While I believe a notchback can be sexy, the GT is a supermodel! It's all about what you want. ||||reg||1
Z||000008||ray b||11-15-2001||04:36 PM||||FORMULA is faster, lighter[so it stops and corners better], and cheaper.<BR>GT looks better but costs more.<BR>88's ride corner and stop better than pre88's.<P>------------------<BR>Question wonder and be wierd||||reg||10
Z||000009||Alex4mula||11-15-2001||04:39 PM||||Basically is a matter of which body you like most. Both have the same soul. Still if you like more the GT it could be cheaper to buy a Formula and change panels to GT. Nice GTs cost over $4K. You can find a nice Formula for over $2K. If you do it yourselve with much less than $2K you can convert.<BR>On the weight side I would like to see a Formula weighting less than 2500lbs. Maybe completly striped and no gas. They are light but not that much.<P>------------------<BR>    Alex4mula<BR>:) Red-Original Owner & White-1/94 with PM (:||||reg||1
Z||000010||Fformula88||11-15-2001||05:17 PM||||MM, you make it sound like the WS6 suspension and V6 drivetrain is a waste of an option!  LOL.  <P>Really, it all comes down to your tastes and what you find.  I was looking for a GT when I bought my Formula.  I like the styling of both (but admit the GT is more sporty looking!).  However, what I found was a lot of beat GT's and a Formula that had a near mint interior and paint job so I took it!  Got a good price on it too, but if I found a GT in as good of shape I would have bought it!<P>They will drive nearly identically, and you can add options to the Formula if you have some "can't live without" options.  But if its a lot of them you might be better off holding out for a GT.<P>Welcome to the forum and good luck hunting for a Fiero!||||reg||1
Z||000011||Monkeyman||11-15-2001||06:36 PM||||Fformula88--You know I'm just about the biggest Fiero nut ever.  That said, I think the WS6 suspension is pretty worthless.  It was a great option when it was new, but unless you have an ultra low mileage car, the suspension will be worn out.  That means that you'll have to replace the parts to make it handle as new.  Might as well upgrade while you're at it.  If you're gonna upgrade, why not spend a little less money (both for the car and the parts) and upgrade an '86/'87?  An upgraded '86/'87 suspension will run circles around a stock '88 WS6 suspension.  I have absolutely nothing against either '88s or Formulas (Formulas just aren't my taste).  That said, if it says 'Fiero' on it (irregardless of year or model), I'll drive it with a grin on my face.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000012||Zoom88||11-15-2001||07:30 PM|||| On a personal note, I prefer the 88 Formula the notchback has a look all its own. I am a 42 year old geyser who has had the fastback look, just about everything out there is a fastback especially those dreadful rice burners, what ever happened to the good ole American muscle car. Their was a little over 5000 formulas built in 88 less than any other model for that year. As an investment, as I have seen so many times over the years with other cars I believe the 88 Formula will eventually become the rarer of the 2 models especially as parts become more and more scarce. As far as stripped down...hmmmm seems most of the High End Sports cars came the same way. <BR> ||||reg||12
Z||000013||rigdonhome||11-15-2001||07:50 PM||||All a matter of opinion, I agree!<P>I've always had a budget with my vehicle projects, but this time I decided to find exactly what I wanted and worry about financing it later.  This is the first time in my life I was able to do so...  I wanted a nice '88 GT so that's what I bought...  They're right (above) about the cost, you'll pay for the GT and more for a nice one.  Mine is almost showroom-condition and was stored for more than 11 years in an environmentally controlled shop, so it is basically a 2-year old car!  <P>Mine was a very rare find for only $5,200 but my point is if it's what you really want do it right.  I had to look all over the US and flew out to the east coast to buy it sight-unseen and drive it back 15 hours.  But I got exactly what I wanted!!!<P>You should do the same.  A Fiero is almost always a 'want' and therefore should be purchased as such.  If budget is an issue and you just need transportation, go with a Geo!<P>Cheers,<BR>Scott<P>------------------<BR>Scott Rigdon<BR>1988 Fiero GT, Black, 5-speed<BR>38,000 Original Miles!<BR>Web Site:   <A HREF="http://www.RigdonHome.com/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.RigdonHome.com/</A> <BR>Story of buying my Fiero:   <A HREF="http://www.RigdonHome.com/cars/88_Fiero_GT/14_hours_in_a_fiero.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.RigdonHome.com/cars/88_Fiero_GT/14_hours_in_a_fiero.htm</A> <BR> <IMG SRC="http://24.249.68.73/cars/88_fiero_gt/rear_signature.jpg"> ||||reg||10
Z||000014||SLYGUY||11-16-2001||04:09 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Turbo88:<P><B>that means you have a .3s advantage<BR> </B><BR><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Oh yeah!!! I'd give up the sexy aero bodywork for performance like that anytime....NOT!!<P>LOL<P>Sly<P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/ViewImage.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000015||ray b||11-16-2001||12:07 PM||||monkeyman my moma used to say you can't make a silk purse out of a soe's ear!!<BR>any pre88 made to handel better than a 88 will ride way worse and a 88 only needs a little help [poly] to be better than a total custom setup on the early car and will all ways ride and stop better.I have owned both and the wife refused to ride in my 87se after riding in the 88.<P>------------------<BR>Question wonder and be wierd||||reg||1
Z||000016||fldevel||11-16-2001||03:22 PM||||do like me .. get one of each .. problem solved  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> ... up to 6 fieros and rising  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000017||Fformula88||11-16-2001||03:40 PM||||MM-Just picking on ya a bit!  You sound a bit harsh on the 88's, but I don't blame you.  I have been becoming increasingly concerned with the outright lack of parts available for the 88 suspensions to the point where thoughts of selling my Formula and finding a 86 or 87 GT have entered my head. For me the fact the parts are disappearing is a much larger concern than the fact that they are a little pricier!<P>Call me crazy, but I really do not think there is a large real world handling difference between the 84-87 and 88 Fieros.  My father has an 85 SE 2m6 with the WS6 option (its not just the 88 suspension!) and as far as going fast down twisty roadsd, both handle exceptionally well.  Neither has had suspensions totally rebuilt, his is totally stock (69K miles, but feels really tight) and mine has new shocks up front, and the previous owner put in poly bushings on the tie rods (84K on my Formula).  The 85 is a little harsher.  Maybe the difference shows up more on the track!  Thats just my seat of the pants opinion.<P>I also totally agree with you.  It only has to say FIERO on the back and I am perfectly happy.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000018||Philphine||11-16-2001||06:07 PM||||i think that formula weight given might have been for a 4cyl coupe. i remember the review for the formula when it came out. i think the difference from a gt was more like 80lbs. ||||reg||1
Z||000019||Spektrum-87GT||11-16-2001||06:35 PM||||go fastback, you wont regret it unless you're going all performance...<P>its similar to the trans am vs. formula(firebird)<P>when it comes down to it, the formula might be faster, but the trans am is the ultimate in looks and options. if cost is what matters, find a gt in some need of work!! they can be had for cheap if you look hard enough.<P>i'm not biased though... really  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>------------------<BR>restoration/modification on hold until who knows when<BR><A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/spektrum_87gt/" TARGET=_blank>87 Fiero GT</A><BR>1991 Jeep Cherokee<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://members.home.com/bspracklin1/sig2.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000020||Fformula88||11-16-2001||08:03 PM||||First off, here are the published curb weights for the 88 Fiero's off of the same Pontiac press release:<P>Coupe 2602<BR>Formula 2743<BR>GT 2790<P>Formula's advantage is 47 lbs, but is generally more do to options.  Since the GT's generally came with a lot more equipment (like power locks and windows, etc) they tend to weigh a bit more as equipped than a Formula.  Does this difference make a really big difference?  Not really unless you have an absolutely stripped Formula and a loaded GT.<P>I also have two sets of track times for the GT and the Formula in 88... all cars were 5 speeds.   Here they are anyway...<P>88 GT #1   0-60 7.8  1/4 not tested<BR>88 GT #2   0-60 8.7  1/4 16.4@81.9<BR>Formula #1 0-60 8.0  1/4 16.0@85.5<BR>Formula #2 0-60 7.9  1/4 16.1@82.0<P>Of course, each was tested at different places by different people with different atmospheric conditions, but they are close that weight shouldn't really effect buying decision.<P>Hey, if we want to get really technical, the Formula's drag coefficient is .357 and the GT's is .365!   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000021||Fierobruiser||11-17-2001||12:12 AM||||Nuff said!<P>------------------<BR>Notchbacks Rule<BR>85GT(show car)<BR>88Coupe (daily driver)<BR>84 Coupe(project car)||||reg||1
